-- The erased generic ABI: a generic body compiled once and called through a runtime environment,
-- rather than cloned per set of type arguments.
--
-- This fixture is compiled with specialization declined (see the `.generic` marker beside it), so
-- the IR below is the erased form. Every other fixture is compiled both ways and its *result*
-- compared, which is Implementation-Generics.md §14's equivalence check; this one exists so that
-- the erased form itself is inspectable.
--
-- What a call site does: build the constant environment for these type arguments - one TypeDesc per
-- slot of the callee's schema - and pass it in a hidden leading position. What the body does: read
-- the size it needs out of that environment instead of out of a type it cannot see.

fn identity(value: a) -> a = value

-- A value of unknown size cannot come back in a register, so an owned generic result goes through
-- storage the caller provides. Substituting `Int` does not change that - the body was compiled once
-- - so the caller allocates, passes the address, and loads the value back out.
fn firstOf(x: a, y: a) -> a = x

-- Two variables, two descriptor slots. The second argument's size is a different slot from the
-- first's, which is what makes the numbering matter rather than being incidental.
fn pick(flag: Bool, x: a, y: b) -> b = y

-- A class the body dispatches on. The instance is not chosen here and never can be: what runs is
-- whatever method table the caller put in the environment, loaded at the slot the schema numbered.
fn (Num(a)) twice(x: a) -> a = x + x

-- A requirement the signature never wrote. `<` records Ord(a) while the body is resolved, and the
-- environment carries a witness for it exactly as if it had been declared.
fn smaller(x: a, y: a) -> a = if x < y then x else y

-- One generic function calling another. `middle` cannot build `smaller`'s environment from concrete
-- types - it has none - so it projects its own slots into the callee's, which is the forwarded case.
fn middle(x: a, y: a, z: a) -> a = smaller(smaller(x, y), z)

-- A literal inside a constrained body. `Num` implies `FromInt`, so the author never wrote it, the
-- diagnostic never mentions it, and it gets no slot either: the witness for it is the one the `Num`
-- witness already names, which is the load the schema below does not have to carry a number for.
fn (Num(a)) increment(x: a) -> a = x + 1

-- A `{}` in a position the callee declared as a variable, which is the case where the two rules for
-- "does this argument exist" disagree. The callee was compiled once, against `a`, so the position is
-- in its signature whatever the caller substituted - it is passed, as the address of the zero bytes
-- `{}` occupies. `3` after it is what makes that worth asserting rather than merely observing: a
-- position dropped for carrying nothing would hand `3` to `x` and leave `y` with nothing at all.
fn carriesNothing() -> Int = pick(True, {}, 3)

-- The same one level in, where the call cannot be specialized even in principle: `a` is `{}` from
-- the literal while `b` is still this body's variable, so it leaves as a deferred generic call. That
-- is the form whose lowering reads the concrete type off the argument to size what it hands over,
-- which an argument that is not there has none of.
fn nothingForwarded(z: b) -> b = pick(False, {}, z)

fn main() -> Int:
    let one = identity(1)
    let two = firstOf(2, 99)
    let three = pick(True, False, 3)

    return one + two + three +
        twice(5) +
        smaller(30, 8) +
        middle(60, 12, 40) +
        increment(19) +
        carriesNothing() +
        nothingForwarded(7)
